lesson: 210
title: Counting back from heads and legs, cycles and wheels
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Cows have 4 legs and chickens 2. If every head belonged to a chicken, there would be 2 legs per head.
explain: Each cow brings 2 more legs than a chicken, so the extra legs divided by 2 give the cows: 10 heads and 28 legs, 10 × 2 = 20, 28 − 20 = 8, 8 ÷ 2 = 4 cows.
explain: Cycles work the same way: a bicycle has 2 wheels and a tricycle 3. 7 cycles with 17 wheels: 17 − 7 × 2 = 3 tricycles.
vars: a=name b=name c=2..12 k=2..15
let: h = c + k
let: l = 4 * c + 2 * k
let: w = 3 * c + 2 * k
answer: c
exercise: A field has cows and chickens, {h} heads and {l} legs in all. How many cows are there?
exercise: A farm has pigs and ducks. {a} counts {h} heads and {l} legs. How many pigs are there?
exercise: In a park there are {h} cycles, bicycles and tricycles, with {w} wheels in all. How many tricycles are there?
exercise: Dogs and chickens play in a yard: {h} heads and {l} legs. How many dogs are in the yard?
exercise: {a} sees goats and geese in a meadow, {h} animals with {l} legs. How many goats are there?
exercise: {b}'s bike shop has {h} bicycles and tricycles with {w} wheels between them. How many tricycles does it have?
exercise: cows n chickens, {h} heads {l} legs. how many cows
exercise: {h} bikes and trikes outside school, {w} wheels, the bell just went. how many trikes
exercise: farm: {h+1} heads, no hang on, {h} heads, {l} legs, sheep n chickens. sheep?
exercise: {a} reckons {h} heads and {l} legs means {h} cows lol. it's cows n ducks. how many cows really
exercise: pigs and hens, counted {h} heads, {l} legs, i'm knackered. pigs?
test: {l} legs and {h} heads were counted in a barn of horses and hens. How many horses are in the barn?
test: Bicycles and tricycles are parked at a fair: {w} wheels on {h} cycles. Find the number of tricycles.
test: How many cows are there, if cows and ducks on a farm have {h} heads and {l} legs together?
test: The goats and chickens in a yard have {l} legs; {h} heads can be seen above the fence. How many goats are there?
test: {h} heads, {l} legs, dogs n geese. dogs how many
test: trikes n bikes at the park, {h} of em, {w} wheels, my little bro wants a trike. how many trikes
exam: It is given that a field contains cows and chickens only, the aggregate number of heads being {h} and the aggregate number of legs {l}. Determine the number of cows.
exam: A parking area accommodates {h} cycles, each either a bicycle or a tricycle, bearing {w} wheels in total. Ascertain the number of tricycles.
exam: Whereas a farmyard contains pigs and ducks, numbering {h} heads and {l} legs collectively, compute the number of pigs therein.
exam: Given that {l} legs and {h} heads have been enumerated among the goats and geese of a meadow, state the number of goats.
exercise: Grandad passed away and left a field of cows and chickens. {a} counts {h} heads and {l} legs. How many cows did he leave?
exercise: {b} was economical with the truth and said there are {h+3} heads. In fact the field of cows and chickens has {h} heads and {l} legs. How many cows are there?
test: fox 'paid a visit' to the hens again, bloody thing. now cows n chickens, {h} heads {l} legs. how many cows
